  "mechanismSummary": "The isopropyl cation, formed with a hydride shift from 1-chloropropane and AlCl₃, is attacked by a ring π bond. AlCl₄⁻ removes the proton. The product is isopropylbenzene (cumene), not propylbenzene: the classic rearrangement trap. The alkylated product is more reactive than benzene, so polyalkylation is a further problem.",
